// Original practice problems for the supplied October 2026 exam scope.
window.CHAPTERS = [
{part:'Part A · Partnership',title:'Partnership fundamentals',short:'Fundamentals',focus:'Appropriation, capital accounts and past adjustments',formula:['Interest on capital = eligible capital × rate × time','Interest on drawings: use actual months outstanding','Guarantee deficiency is borne in the agreed ratio'],questions:[
{title:'Appropriation with a guarantee',marks:8,tags:['P&L Appropriation','Guarantee'],prompt:`A and B share profits 3:2. Their fixed capitals are ₹4,00,000 and ₹3,00,000. The deed allows 10% interest on capital, a salary of ₹6,000 per month to A, and 12% interest on drawings. A withdrew ₹5,000 at the end of each month; B withdrew ₹60,000 on 1 October. B is guaranteed a minimum ₹1,20,000 share of the residual divisible profit, with any deficiency borne by A. Profit before these adjustments is ₹3,20,000 for the year ended 31 March 2026. Prepare the Profit and Loss Appropriation Account and find each partner's final share.`,steps:['Interest on drawings: A = ₹5,000 × 12 × 5.5/12 × 12% = ₹3,300; B = ₹60,000 × 6/12 × 12% = ₹3,600. Credit total ₹6,900 to P&L Appropriation.','Available amount = ₹3,20,000 + ₹6,900 = ₹3,26,900. Interest on capital: A ₹40,000, B ₹30,000. A salary ₹72,000. Residual profit = ₹1,84,900.','Residual 3:2 shares: A ₹1,10,940; B ₹73,960. B deficiency = ₹1,20,000 − ₹73,960 = ₹46,040, transferred from A. Final residual shares: A ₹64,900; B ₹1,20,000.','Appropriation debit side: capital interest ₹70,000; A salary ₹72,000; final profit shares ₹1,84,900. Total ₹3,26,900.']},
{title:'Fixed capital and current accounts',marks:8,tags:['Capital accounts','Drawings'],prompt:`X and Y have fixed capitals of ₹2,50,000 and ₹2,00,000 and share profits 3:2. At 1 April, their current accounts show credit balances of ₹18,000 and ₹9,000. During the year, X drew ₹4,000 at the beginning of each quarter; Y drew ₹3,000 at the end of each month. Interest on capital is 8%; interest on drawings is 10%. X receives a salary of ₹24,000. Profit before appropriation is ₹1,50,000. Prepare both current accounts and explain why fixed capital balances remain unchanged.`,steps:['Interest on capital: X ₹20,000; Y ₹16,000. Interest on drawings: X ₹16,000 × 7.5/12 × 10% = ₹1,000; Y ₹36,000 × 5.5/12 × 10% = ₹1,650.','Residual = ₹1,50,000 + ₹2,650 − ₹36,000 − ₹24,000 = ₹92,650. X gets ₹55,590 and Y ₹37,060.','X current closing credit = ₹18,000 + ₹20,000 + ₹24,000 + ₹55,590 − ₹16,000 − ₹1,000 = ₹1,00,590. Y = ₹9,000 + ₹16,000 + ₹37,060 − ₹36,000 − ₹1,650 = ₹24,410.','Under the fixed-capital method, routine appropriations and drawings go through current accounts; fixed capitals change only when capital is expressly added or withdrawn.']},
{title:'Past adjustment after an omitted interest clause',marks:6,tags:['Past adjustment','Journal entry'],prompt:`P, Q and R shared last year's ₹2,40,000 profit 2:2:1. After closing the books they discover the deed required 10% interest on capitals of ₹3,00,000, ₹2,00,000 and ₹1,00,000 respectively, and a ₹20,000 salary for Q. No correcting entry has been passed. Work out one adjustment entry without reopening the Profit and Loss Appropriation Account.`,steps:['Correct appropriation: capital interest P ₹30,000, Q ₹20,000, R ₹10,000; Q salary ₹20,000. Residual = ₹1,60,000; split 2:2:1 gives P ₹64,000, Q ₹64,000, R ₹32,000.','Correct totals: P ₹94,000; Q ₹1,04,000; R ₹42,000. Amounts already credited from ₹2,40,000: P ₹96,000; Q ₹96,000; R ₹48,000.','P is overcredited ₹2,000 and R ₹6,000; Q is undercredited ₹8,000. Entry: P’s Capital/Current A/c Dr ₹2,000; R’s Capital/Current A/c Dr ₹6,000; To Q’s Capital/Current A/c ₹8,000.']}
]},
{part:'Part A · Partnership',title:'Goodwill: nature and valuation',short:'Goodwill',focus:'Weighted average, super profit and capitalisation',formula:['Average profit × years’ purchase','Super profit = maintainable profit − normal profit','Capitalised goodwill = capitalised business value − capital employed'],questions:[
{title:'Weighted average after abnormal items',marks:8,tags:['Weighted average','Adjustments'],prompt:`A firm earned profits of ₹1,20,000, ₹1,50,000, ₹1,80,000 and ₹2,10,000 in four years. The earliest year includes an abnormal loss of ₹20,000; the latest includes a non-recurring gain of ₹30,000. Apply weights 1, 2, 3 and 4 respectively. Value goodwill at three years’ purchase of weighted average maintainable profit. Also show the unweighted adjusted average as a check.`,steps:['Adjusted profits are ₹1,40,000, ₹1,50,000, ₹1,80,000 and ₹1,80,000. Remove abnormal items before applying weights.','Weighted total = ₹1,40,000×1 + ₹1,50,000×2 + ₹1,80,000×3 + ₹1,80,000×4 = ₹17,00,000. Sum of weights = 10.','Weighted average = ₹1,70,000; goodwill = ₹1,70,000 × 3 = ₹5,10,000. Unweighted adjusted average = ₹6,50,000/4 = ₹1,62,500.']},
{title:'Super profit with a corrected capital base',marks:8,tags:['Super profit','Normal return'],prompt:`Capital employed is ₹8,00,000 and normal return is 12%. Profits over four years are ₹1,40,000, ₹1,60,000, ₹1,20,000 and ₹1,80,000. The second year includes an abnormal gain of ₹20,000, and the third includes an abnormal loss of ₹30,000. Estimate goodwill at four years’ purchase of average super profit.`,steps:['Adjusted profits: ₹1,40,000, ₹1,40,000, ₹1,50,000 and ₹1,80,000. Average maintainable profit = ₹6,10,000/4 = ₹1,52,500.','Normal profit = ₹8,00,000 × 12% = ₹96,000. Average super profit = ₹1,52,500 − ₹96,000 = ₹56,500.','Goodwill = ₹56,500 × 4 = ₹2,26,000.']},
{title:'Capitalisation of average profits',marks:6,tags:['Capitalisation','Goodwill'],prompt:`A firm has maintainable profits of ₹2,40,000. A comparable business earns a 15% normal return. Its tangible net assets are ₹12,50,000 and no other intangible assets are recorded. Calculate capitalised value, goodwill by the capitalisation method, and the implied super profit. Explain how this differs from choosing an arbitrary years’ purchase.`,steps:['Capitalised value of business = ₹2,40,000 ÷ 15% = ₹16,00,000.','Goodwill = capitalised value − tangible net assets = ₹16,00,000 − ₹12,50,000 = ₹3,50,000.','Normal profit on actual net assets = ₹12,50,000 × 15% = ₹1,87,500; super profit = ₹52,500. Capitalisation converts the profit stream at a required return; a years’ purchase multiplies super profit by an assumed number of years.']}
]},
{part:'Part A · Partnership',title:'Change in profit sharing ratio',short:'Ratio change',focus:'Sacrifice, gaining and revaluation',formula:['Sacrifice = old share − new share','Gain = new share − old share','Gaining partners compensate sacrificing partners'],questions:[
{title:'Goodwill and reserves on a ratio change',marks:8,tags:['Sacrificing ratio','Goodwill'],prompt:`A, B and C share profits 5:3:2 and decide to share 2:2:1. Goodwill is valued at ₹2,00,000 but is not to remain in the books. The books show a General Reserve of ₹1,00,000. Calculate each partner’s gain or sacrifice and pass the adjustment entries for goodwill and the reserve.`,steps:['Old shares: A 1/2, B 3/10, C 1/5. New shares: A 2/5, B 2/5, C 1/5. A sacrifices 1/10, B gains 1/10, C is unchanged.','B compensates A by ₹2,00,000 × 1/10 = ₹20,000: B’s Capital A/c Dr ₹20,000; To A’s Capital A/c ₹20,000. No goodwill asset is raised.','Reserve belongs to the old partners: General Reserve A/c Dr ₹1,00,000; To A’s Capital ₹50,000; To B’s Capital ₹30,000; To C’s Capital ₹20,000.']},
{title:'Revaluation and accumulated loss',marks:8,tags:['Revaluation','Old ratio'],prompt:`P, Q and R share 3:2:1 and change to 2:2:2. Their machinery is undervalued by ₹60,000; stock is overvalued by ₹18,000; a ₹12,000 unrecorded creditor exists. The Balance Sheet also contains a debit balance of ₹30,000 in Profit and Loss Account. Prepare the revaluation result, allocate it, and write off the accumulated loss.`,steps:['Revaluation credit ₹60,000; debits ₹18,000 and ₹12,000. Profit = ₹30,000. Credit P ₹15,000, Q ₹10,000, R ₹5,000 in the old 3:2:1 ratio.','Write off accumulated loss ₹30,000 in old ratio: debit P ₹15,000, Q ₹10,000, R ₹5,000; credit Profit and Loss A/c ₹30,000.','Net capital impact from these two items is zero for each partner, but the two entries must still be shown separately.']},
{title:'Memorandum revaluation when book values stay',marks:8,tags:['Memorandum revaluation','Gaining ratio'],prompt:`A and B share 3:2 and decide to share equally. Equipment is worth ₹40,000 more and a liability is ₹10,000 understated. They agree not to change asset and liability book values. Prepare the two parts of a Memorandum Revaluation Account and give the net capital adjustment.`,steps:['First part recognises a ₹30,000 revaluation profit, distributed in old 3:2 ratio: A ₹18,000 and B ₹12,000.','Reverse the two revaluations in the second part to restore book values. The resulting ₹30,000 loss is allocated in the new 1:1 ratio: A ₹15,000 and B ₹15,000.','Net: A’s capital credited ₹3,000; B’s capital debited ₹3,000. Final equipment and liability balances stay at their original book values.']}
]},
{part:'Part A · Partnership',title:'Admission of a partner',short:'Admission',focus:'New ratio, premium, revaluation and capitals',formula:['New partner’s goodwill premium = firm goodwill × incoming share','Sacrificing partners receive premium in sacrificing ratio','New partner’s capital may imply total firm capital'],questions:[
{title:'Admission with goodwill and revaluation',marks:10,tags:['Admission','Revaluation'],prompt:`A and B share 3:2. C is admitted for 1/4, acquired from A and B in 2:1, and brings ₹1,50,000 capital plus ₹60,000 premium for goodwill. Machinery rises by ₹40,000, stock falls by ₹10,000, and an unrecorded creditor of ₹5,000 is recognised. General Reserve is ₹50,000. Calculate the new ratio and pass the principal capital adjustments.`,steps:['A sacrifices 1/6; B sacrifices 1/12. New shares: A 3/5−1/6 = 13/30; B 2/5−1/12 = 19/60; C 1/4 = 15/60. Ratio = 26:19:15.','Goodwill premium ₹60,000 is credited to A and B in sacrificing ratio 2:1: A ₹40,000, B ₹20,000. C brings cash ₹2,10,000 total.','Revaluation profit = ₹40,000−₹10,000−₹5,000 = ₹25,000. Credit old partners 3:2: A ₹15,000, B ₹10,000.','General Reserve ₹50,000 is credited to A ₹30,000 and B ₹20,000 in old ratio.']},
{title:'Hidden goodwill from the incoming capital',marks:8,tags:['Hidden goodwill','Capital adjustment'],prompt:`P and Q share 3:2. Their adjusted capitals immediately before admission are ₹3,60,000 and ₹2,40,000. R is admitted for 1/5 and contributes ₹2,00,000 as capital only. All three agree that total firm capital should be based on R’s contribution. Find hidden goodwill and explain the direction of the adjustment if R’s share is taken equally from P and Q.`,steps:['Implied total capital = ₹2,00,000 ÷ 1/5 = ₹10,00,000. Actual combined capital after R contributes = ₹3,60,000 + ₹2,40,000 + ₹2,00,000 = ₹8,00,000.','Hidden goodwill = ₹2,00,000. R’s share of goodwill = ₹2,00,000 × 1/5 = ₹40,000.','If sacrifice is equal, R’s Capital A/c Dr ₹40,000; To P’s Capital A/c ₹20,000; To Q’s Capital A/c ₹20,000. This is a capital adjustment; R did not bring separate cash premium.']},
{title:'Admission balance sheet assembly',marks:10,tags:['Balance sheet','New capital'],prompt:`A and B share 3:2. Their Balance Sheet shows capitals A ₹3,00,000, B ₹2,00,000; creditors ₹1,00,000; cash ₹50,000; stock ₹1,50,000; machinery ₹4,00,000. C joins for 1/4 and brings ₹2,00,000 capital and ₹40,000 goodwill premium, shared by A and B in old ratio. Stock increases by ₹20,000 and machinery falls by ₹10,000. Creditors include an excess provision of ₹5,000. A and B withdraw the entire goodwill premium in cash. Prepare the new Balance Sheet totals and capital balances.`,steps:['Revaluation profit = ₹20,000−₹10,000+₹5,000 = ₹15,000; credit A ₹9,000 and B ₹6,000. Goodwill premium credits A ₹24,000 and B ₹16,000, then their withdrawals reverse those increases.','Closing capitals: A ₹3,09,000; B ₹2,06,000; C ₹2,00,000. Creditors fall to ₹95,000. Total liabilities and capitals = ₹8,10,000.','Cash = ₹50,000 + ₹2,40,000 − ₹40,000 = ₹2,50,000; stock ₹1,70,000; machinery ₹3,90,000. Total assets = ₹8,10,000.']}
]},
{part:'Part A · Partnership',title:'Retirement of a partner',short:'Retirement',focus:'Gaining ratio, goodwill and settlement',formula:['Gaining ratio = new share − old share','Retiring partner receives goodwill from gaining partners','Amount due can be transferred to loan account'],questions:[
{title:'Retirement with revaluation and loan',marks:10,tags:['Retirement','Partner loan'],prompt:`A, B and C share 3:2:1. C retires; A and B will share 3:2. Capitals are A ₹3,00,000, B ₹2,00,000 and C ₹1,50,000. General Reserve is ₹60,000. Goodwill is ₹1,20,000 and will not appear in the books. Machinery increases by ₹30,000, stock falls by ₹12,000 and ₹6,000 of creditors are no longer payable. C is paid ₹80,000 immediately; the rest becomes a loan. Find C’s total claim and the loan.`,steps:['A old 1/2 to new 3/5 gains 1/10; B old 1/3 to new 2/5 gains 1/15. Gaining ratio = 3:2. C’s goodwill = ₹1,20,000 × 1/6 = ₹20,000; debit A ₹12,000 and B ₹8,000, credit C ₹20,000.','Revaluation profit = ₹30,000−₹12,000+₹6,000 = ₹24,000; C’s old-ratio share = ₹4,000. C’s General Reserve share = ₹10,000.','C’s claim = capital ₹1,50,000 + reserve ₹10,000 + revaluation ₹4,000 + goodwill ₹20,000 = ₹1,84,000. Loan after ₹80,000 cash payment = ₹1,04,000.']},
{title:'Retiring partner’s share and capitals',marks:8,tags:['Gaining ratio','Capitals'],prompt:`P, Q and R share 4:3:2. R retires and P and Q agree to share 5:4. Goodwill is ₹1,80,000 and is adjusted through capitals. The balance sheet has a debit Profit and Loss balance of ₹45,000 and an Investment Fluctuation Reserve of ₹27,000 with no loss in investment value. Find the gaining ratio and all three partner capital effects before settlement.`,steps:['Old shares P 4/9, Q 3/9, R 2/9; new P 5/9, Q 4/9. P and Q each gain 1/9, so gaining ratio = 1:1.','R’s goodwill share = ₹1,80,000 × 2/9 = ₹40,000. Debit P ₹20,000 and Q ₹20,000; credit R ₹40,000.','Debit P&L loss ₹45,000 in old 4:3:2: P ₹20,000, Q ₹15,000, R ₹10,000. Credit reserve ₹27,000 in old ratio: P ₹12,000, Q ₹9,000, R ₹6,000.','Net capital changes: P −₹28,000; Q −₹26,000; R +₹36,000.']},
{title:'Settlement with interest on retiring partner loan',marks:6,tags:['Loan account','Interest'],prompt:`On 1 April 2025, ₹2,40,000 due to a retiring partner is transferred to her Loan Account. The deed says 10% interest and equal annual principal repayments of ₹80,000 each 31 March, with interest paid alongside each instalment. Prepare the loan balance and interest for the three years ending 31 March 2028.`,steps:['2025–26: opening loan ₹2,40,000; interest ₹24,000; cash paid ₹1,04,000 (₹80,000 principal + interest); closing loan ₹1,60,000.','2026–27: interest ₹16,000; cash paid ₹96,000; closing loan ₹80,000.','2027–28: interest ₹8,000; cash paid ₹88,000; closing loan nil. Total interest paid ₹48,000.']}
]},
{part:'Part A · Partnership',title:'Death of a partner',short:'Death',focus:'Profit to date of death, goodwill and executor',formula:['Time-based profit = prior profit × months/12 × deceased share','Sales-based profit = prior profit × current sales/prior sales × deceased share','Executor claim includes capital plus entitlements less drawings'],questions:[
{title:'Executor’s account with time-based profit',marks:10,tags:['Executor','Profit to date'],prompt:`A, B and C share 2:2:1. C dies on 30 September 2025. His capital at 1 April is ₹2,00,000. Last year’s profit was ₹3,00,000; current profit to death is estimated by time. Goodwill is valued at ₹2,50,000. C’s drawings to death were ₹15,000; interest on drawings is ₹500. He is entitled to 8% interest on capital up to death. General Reserve is ₹50,000. A and B take over C’s share equally and pay ₹1,00,000 immediately. Compute the executor’s claim and outstanding loan.`,steps:['C’s share of estimated profit = ₹3,00,000 × 6/12 × 1/5 = ₹30,000. Capital interest = ₹2,00,000 × 8% × 6/12 = ₹8,000.','C’s goodwill share = ₹2,50,000 × 1/5 = ₹50,000, borne ₹25,000 each by A and B. Reserve share = ₹50,000 × 1/5 = ₹10,000.','Executor claim = ₹2,00,000 + ₹30,000 + ₹8,000 + ₹50,000 + ₹10,000 − ₹15,000 − ₹500 = ₹2,82,500. Outstanding after immediate payment = ₹1,82,500.']},
{title:'Profit to death using sales',marks:8,tags:['Sales basis','Profit to date'],prompt:`P, Q and R share profits 3:2:1. Q dies on 31 December. Last full year’s sales and profit were ₹24,00,000 and ₹3,60,000. Sales from 1 April to 31 December are ₹21,00,000. The deed uses sales to estimate interim profit. Q had a ₹6,000 monthly salary and drew ₹4,000 per month at each month-end until death. Compute Q’s salary, estimated profit share and drawings to be posted to his capital or current account.`,steps:['Past profit margin = ₹3,60,000/₹24,00,000 = 15%. Estimated interim profit = ₹21,00,000 × 15% = ₹3,15,000.','Q’s share = 2/6 = 1/3, so profit to death = ₹1,05,000. Salary for nine months = ₹54,000.','Drawings = ₹4,000 × 9 = ₹36,000, debited to Q. Net effect of these three items on Q’s account is +₹1,23,000.']},
{title:'Interest on executor loan after death',marks:6,tags:['Executor loan','Settlement'],prompt:`After all death adjustments, ₹3,00,000 is due to a deceased partner’s executor on 1 July 2025. The deed provides 9% annual interest on the unpaid balance. The firm pays ₹1,20,000 of principal on 31 December 2025 and clears the balance with interest on 31 March 2026. Prepare the interest and cash payment timeline.`,steps:['1 July–31 December: interest on ₹3,00,000 for six months = ₹13,500. If paid with the instalment, 31 December cash payment is ₹1,33,500.','Remaining principal ₹1,80,000. 1 January–31 March: interest for three months = ₹4,050. Final cash payment = ₹1,84,050.','Total interest = ₹17,550. State interest-payment timing in the working if the question is silent; principal balances are unaffected.']}
]},
{part:'Part A · Partnership',title:'Dissolution of a partnership firm',short:'Dissolution',focus:'Realisation, expenses and final settlement',formula:['Transfer non-cash assets to Realisation debit','Transfer external liabilities to Realisation credit','Realisation profit or loss goes to partner capitals in profit ratio'],questions:[
{title:'Realisation account and final settlement',marks:10,tags:['Realisation','Final cash'],prompt:`A and B share 3:2. Their Balance Sheet at dissolution shows capitals A ₹2,00,000 and B ₹1,50,000; creditors ₹80,000; bank ₹30,000; stock ₹1,00,000; debtors ₹1,20,000; plant ₹1,80,000. Stock realises ₹85,000, debtors ₹1,10,000 and plant ₹1,60,000. Creditors settle at ₹75,000 and realisation expenses paid are ₹10,000. Prepare the Realisation Account result and final payments to partners.`,steps:['Realisation debit: transferred assets ₹4,00,000, creditor settlement ₹75,000, expenses ₹10,000 = ₹4,85,000. Credit: creditors ₹80,000 + sale proceeds ₹3,55,000 = ₹4,35,000. Loss = ₹50,000.','Loss borne A ₹30,000 and B ₹20,000. Capital claims after loss: A ₹1,70,000; B ₹1,30,000.','Bank available = opening ₹30,000 + sale proceeds ₹3,55,000 − creditors ₹75,000 − expenses ₹10,000 = ₹3,00,000; pay A ₹1,70,000 and B ₹1,30,000.']},
{title:'Partner takes over an asset and liability',marks:8,tags:['Takeover','Realisation'],prompt:`X and Y share profits equally. At dissolution, machinery has book value ₹1,20,000, stock ₹80,000, debtors ₹50,000 and creditors ₹70,000. X takes over machinery at ₹1,00,000 and agrees to pay creditors personally at ₹65,000. Stock sells for ₹72,000; debtors realise ₹45,000; expenses of ₹6,000 are paid by the firm. Find the Realisation Account profit or loss and specify the entry effect on X’s capital.`,steps:['Debit Realisation with assets ₹2,50,000, expenses ₹6,000 and liability takeover by X ₹65,000. Credit it with creditors transferred ₹70,000, machinery takeover ₹1,00,000, stock proceeds ₹72,000 and debtors realised ₹45,000.','Total credit ₹2,87,000; total debit ₹3,21,000; realisation loss ₹34,000, shared X ₹17,000 and Y ₹17,000.','X’s capital is debited ₹1,00,000 for machinery and credited ₹65,000 for taking over creditors; his ₹17,000 loss share is another debit. Net capital effect = −₹52,000. X personally settles the creditor; the firm makes no bank payment for it.']},
{title:'Insolvent partner in a dissolution',marks:8,tags:['Insolvency','Capital deficiency'],prompt:`After the Realisation Account has been closed, A and B’s capitals are credit ₹90,000 and ₹60,000 while C’s capital is debit ₹30,000. They share 3:2:1. C can bring in only ₹6,000. The partnership deed is silent on insolvency. Apply the Garner v Murray principle, stating the assumption about A and B’s last agreed capitals, which are ₹1,20,000 and ₹80,000. Find final claims before any cash distribution.`,steps:['C pays ₹6,000, leaving a ₹24,000 deficiency. Under Garner v Murray, solvent partners bear it in last agreed capital ratio ₹1,20,000:₹80,000 = 3:2, assuming the principle applies.','Debit A ₹14,400 and B ₹9,600; credit C ₹24,000, clearing C’s account after the ₹6,000 cash receipt.','Final claims: A ₹75,600; B ₹50,400. Together they equal ₹1,26,000. If the exam specifies a different local rule or capital basis, use that stated rule.']}
]},
{part:'Part A · Company',title:'Issue of shares',short:'Shares',focus:'Pro rata allotment, forfeiture and reissue',formula:['Pro rata excess application money is adjusted against allotment','Forfeited shares credit = amount received toward share capital','Capital reserve on reissue = related forfeiture balance − reissue discount'],questions:[
{title:'Oversubscription and pro rata allotment',marks:10,tags:['Pro rata','Journal entries'],prompt:`A company issues 20,000 equity shares of ₹10 each at a premium of ₹2, payable ₹3 on application, ₹5 on allotment (including premium), and ₹4 on first and final call. Applications arrive for 30,000 shares. Applications for 6,000 shares are rejected; the remaining applicants receive 20,000 shares pro rata. All money is received. Prepare the main journal amounts and show how excess application money is treated.`,steps:['Application cash received = 30,000×₹3 = ₹90,000. Refund rejected applications = 6,000×₹3 = ₹18,000. Required application on allotted shares = 20,000×₹3 = ₹60,000. Excess from accepted applicants = ₹12,000, adjusted to allotment.','Allotment due = 20,000×₹5 = ₹1,00,000: credit Share Capital ₹60,000 and Securities Premium ₹40,000. Cash due after application excess = ₹88,000.','Call due and received = 20,000×₹4 = ₹80,000. Share Capital total = ₹2,00,000; Securities Premium = ₹40,000; net cash retained ₹2,40,000.']},
{title:'Forfeiture and reissue with premium unpaid',marks:10,tags:['Forfeiture','Reissue'],prompt:`A company issued 5,000 shares of ₹10 each at ₹2 premium, payable ₹3 application, ₹5 allotment (including premium) and ₹4 final call. Holder X, with 400 shares, paid application only; holder Y, with 300 shares, paid application and allotment but missed the call. Their shares were forfeited. Later 500 of the forfeited shares—300 from X and 200 from Y—were reissued fully paid for ₹8 each. Pass forfeiture and reissue entries and calculate the related Capital Reserve.`,steps:['X forfeiture: debit Share Capital ₹4,000 and Securities Premium ₹800 (premium was due but unpaid); credit Share Allotment ₹2,000, Share Final Call ₹1,600 and Share Forfeiture ₹1,200.','Y forfeiture: debit Share Capital ₹3,000; credit Share Final Call ₹1,200 and Share Forfeiture ₹1,800. Premium was received, so do not reverse it.','Reissue: Bank Dr ₹4,000; Share Forfeiture Dr ₹1,000; To Share Capital ₹5,000. Discount ₹2×500 is within forfeiture available on those specific shares.','Forfeiture attributable to reissued shares: X 300×₹3 = ₹900; Y 200×₹6 = ₹1,200; total ₹2,100. Transfer ₹1,100 remaining after ₹1,000 discount to Capital Reserve.']},
{title:'Calls in advance and calls in arrears',marks:8,tags:['Calls','Interest'],prompt:`A company has 10,000 shares of ₹10 fully called, with a final call of ₹3 payable on 1 October 2025. A holder of 500 shares pays this call three months early. A different holder of 400 shares pays it on 31 December 2025, three months late. Articles permit interest at 12% on calls in advance and 10% on calls in arrears. All other calls are paid on time. Calculate the call cash flow and both interest amounts.`,steps:['Final call due = 10,000×₹3 = ₹30,000. Calls in advance received earlier = 500×₹3 = ₹1,500; arrears at due date = 400×₹3 = ₹1,200. Cash collected on due date from others = ₹27,300.','Interest on calls in advance = ₹1,500×12%×3/12 = ₹45, an expense. Interest on arrears = ₹1,200×10%×3/12 = ₹30, income when recovered.','31 December collection from late holder = ₹1,200 principal + ₹30 interest. Total principal call receipts across the three dates = ₹30,000.']}
]},
{part:'Part B · Analysis',title:'Accounting ratios',short:'Ratios',focus:'Liquidity, turnover, solvency and profitability',formula:['Current ratio = current assets/current liabilities','Quick assets = current assets − inventory − prepaid expenses','ROCE = EBIT/average capital employed × 100'],questions:[
{title:'Liquidity after two transactions',marks:8,tags:['Current ratio','Quick ratio'],prompt:`A firm has current assets ₹6,00,000, including inventory ₹2,00,000 and prepaid expenses ₹20,000. Current liabilities are ₹3,00,000. It pays ₹60,000 of creditors in cash, then buys inventory worth ₹80,000 on credit. Compute current and quick ratios before and after both transactions, and explain the movement.`,steps:['Initially current ratio = ₹6,00,000/₹3,00,000 = 2:1. Quick assets = ₹3,80,000; quick ratio = 1.27:1 (rounded).','After creditor payment: CA ₹5,40,000; CL ₹2,40,000; inventory ₹2,00,000; quick assets ₹3,20,000. Ratios = 2.25:1 and 1.33:1.','After credit inventory purchase: CA ₹6,20,000; CL ₹3,20,000; inventory ₹2,80,000; quick assets still ₹3,20,000. Final ratios = 1.94:1 and 1:1.']},
{title:'Turnover and collection period',marks:8,tags:['Turnover','Working capital'],prompt:`Revenue from operations is ₹24,00,000, of which 20% is cash sales. Opening and closing trade receivables are ₹2,40,000 and ₹3,60,000. Cost of revenue is ₹15,00,000; opening and closing inventory are ₹2,00,000 and ₹3,00,000. Calculate receivables turnover, average collection period (365 days), inventory turnover and average holding period.`,steps:['Credit sales = ₹24,00,000×80% = ₹19,20,000. Average receivables = ₹3,00,000. Receivables turnover = 6.4 times; collection period = 365/6.4 ≈ 57.0 days.','Average inventory = ₹2,50,000. Inventory turnover = ₹15,00,000/₹2,50,000 = 6 times; holding period = 365/6 ≈ 60.8 days.','Use credit revenue for receivables turnover and cost of revenue for inventory turnover; mixing those bases distorts the interpretation.']},
{title:'Solvency and profitability from one data set',marks:10,tags:['Debt-equity','ROCE'],prompt:`A company has equity share capital ₹8,00,000, reserves ₹2,00,000, long-term borrowings ₹6,00,000 and current liabilities ₹4,00,000. EBIT is ₹2,40,000, finance cost ₹60,000, tax ₹45,000 and revenue from operations ₹20,00,000. Assume capital employed is shareholders’ funds plus long-term borrowings, and average equals closing capital employed. Compute debt-equity ratio, interest coverage, net profit ratio and ROCE.`,steps:['Shareholders’ funds = ₹10,00,000. Debt-equity ratio = ₹6,00,000/₹10,00,000 = 0.6:1.','Interest coverage = EBIT/finance cost = ₹2,40,000/₹60,000 = 4 times. Profit after tax = ₹2,40,000−₹60,000−₹45,000 = ₹1,35,000.','Net profit ratio = ₹1,35,000/₹20,00,000 ×100 = 6.75%. Capital employed = ₹16,00,000; ROCE = ₹2,40,000/₹16,00,000 ×100 = 15%.']}
]},
{part:'Part B · Analysis',title:'Financial statements of a company',short:'Company statements',focus:'Schedule III classification and statement assembly',formula:['Current assets and liabilities turn over or settle in the operating cycle or 12 months','Shareholders’ funds = share capital + reserves and surplus','Balance Sheet: assets = equity + liabilities'],questions:[
{title:'Classify a mixed list into the Balance Sheet',marks:8,tags:['Schedule III','Classification'],prompt:`Classify these items under the major and appropriate subheadings of a company Balance Sheet: equity share capital ₹8,00,000; securities premium ₹1,20,000; 5-year bank loan ₹3,00,000; trade payables ₹90,000; outstanding salaries ₹20,000; machinery ₹7,00,000; long-term investments ₹2,00,000; inventory ₹1,50,000; trade receivables ₹1,80,000; cash ₹1,00,000. Identify whether the list balances and, if not, the amount of a missing asset or liability.`,steps:['Equity and liabilities: Shareholders’ funds—share capital ₹8,00,000, reserves and surplus/securities premium ₹1,20,000; non-current liabilities—long-term borrowing ₹3,00,000; current liabilities—trade payables ₹90,000, other current liabilities/outstanding salaries ₹20,000. Total ₹13,30,000.','Assets: non-current—property, plant and equipment/machinery ₹7,00,000, non-current investments ₹2,00,000; current—inventory ₹1,50,000, trade receivables ₹1,80,000, cash ₹1,00,000. Total ₹13,30,000.','The list balances exactly; no missing item. State assumptions if the maturity or operating-cycle status of a receivable is changed in a variant.']},
{title:'Statement of Profit and Loss with tax',marks:8,tags:['Statement of P&L','Tax'],prompt:`For the year ended 31 March, a company reports revenue from operations ₹25,00,000, other income ₹80,000, cost of materials consumed ₹10,00,000, employee benefit expense ₹5,00,000, finance costs ₹60,000, depreciation ₹1,20,000 and other expenses ₹4,00,000. Current tax is ₹1,20,000; deferred tax expense is ₹20,000. Prepare the main Statement of Profit and Loss totals and profit after tax.`,steps:['Total income = revenue ₹25,00,000 + other income ₹80,000 = ₹25,80,000.','Total expenses before tax = ₹10,00,000+₹5,00,000+₹60,000+₹1,20,000+₹4,00,000 = ₹20,80,000. Profit before tax = ₹5,00,000.','Tax expense = current tax ₹1,20,000 + deferred tax ₹20,000 = ₹1,40,000. Profit after tax = ₹3,60,000.']},
{title:'Current versus non-current classification',marks:6,tags:['Classification','Operating cycle'],prompt:`A company’s operating cycle is 15 months. It holds inventory expected to sell in 13 months, trade receivables expected to collect in 14 months, a freely withdrawable 10-month bank deposit, a term loan payable in 30 months, and the ₹50,000 instalment of that loan due in eight months. Classify each, explaining the role of the operating cycle and the current portion of long-term debt.`,steps:['Inventory and trade receivables are current because they are realised in the normal 15-month operating cycle, even though each exceeds 12 months.','The freely withdrawable 10-month bank deposit is a current asset, generally shown under other bank balances. Its original maturity exceeds three months, so it is not normally a cash equivalent.','The term loan balance payable in 30 months is non-current; the ₹50,000 due in eight months is its current maturity and appears in current liabilities.']}
]},
{part:'Part B · Analysis',title:'Financial statement analysis',short:'Statement analysis',focus:'Reading trends and limits of reported figures',formula:['Absolute change = current year − prior year','Trend index = current amount/base year amount × 100','Interpret changes together with margins, cash and context'],questions:[
{title:'A trend that hides margin pressure',marks:8,tags:['Trend analysis','Interpretation'],prompt:`Revenue rose from ₹20 lakh to ₹25 lakh and then ₹30 lakh over three years. Profit after tax moved from ₹2 lakh to ₹2.2 lakh to ₹2.1 lakh. Use Year 1 as base 100 to calculate trend percentages for both series and net profit margins. Write a short interpretation, including one further figure you would request.`,steps:['Revenue trend = 100, 125, 150. Profit trend = 100, 110, 105.','Net profit margin = 10%, 8.8%, 7% respectively. Revenue is growing while conversion to bottom-line profit is weakening.','Ask for gross margin, finance cost or expense breakdown, and operating cash flow to test whether cost pressure, financing or collections explain the pattern. The trends alone do not prove the cause.']},
{title:'Compare two companies without confusing size',marks:8,tags:['Analysis','Ratios'],prompt:`Company A has sales ₹100 crore, profit ₹5 crore, assets ₹80 crore and debt ₹40 crore. Company B has sales ₹40 crore, profit ₹4 crore, assets ₹20 crore and debt ₹4 crore. Compare profit margin, return on assets and debt-to-assets for both. Explain which conclusions are supportable and which need more information.`,steps:['A: profit margin 5%; ROA 6.25%; debt-to-assets 50%. B: margin 10%; ROA 20%; debt-to-assets 20%.','A is larger in sales and absolute profit; B is stronger on these efficiency/profitability measures and less debt-funded by this simple measure.','Do not call B unconditionally “better”: asset age, accounting policies, one-off items, industry, cash flows and risk must be checked.']},
{title:'Working capital warning from statements',marks:8,tags:['Liquidity','Interpretation'],prompt:`A company’s revenue grows 20% from ₹10 crore to ₹12 crore. Trade receivables rise from ₹1 crore to ₹2 crore, inventory from ₹80 lakh to ₹1.5 crore, while trade payables rise from ₹70 lakh to ₹1 crore. Estimate receivables as a percentage of revenue and net operating working capital (receivables + inventory − payables) for both years. Explain the warning and one plausible benign explanation.`,steps:['Receivables/revenue: Year 1 = 10%; Year 2 = 16.7%. Net operating working capital: Year 1 ₹1.10 crore; Year 2 ₹2.50 crore.','Working capital tied up rises ₹1.40 crore, much faster than sales. This may pressure operating cash flow even if profit rises.','A benign explanation could be deliberate inventory buildup before a seasonal sales peak or changed credit terms for a large reliable customer; check ageing and subsequent collections.']}
]},
{part:'Part B · Analysis',title:'Comparative and common-size statements',short:'Comparative & common-size',focus:'Base amounts, percentage changes and vertical analysis',formula:['Comparative % change = (current − previous)/previous × 100','Common-size P&L: every line ÷ revenue × 100','Common-size Balance Sheet: every line ÷ total assets or total equity and liabilities × 100'],questions:[
{title:'Comparative Statement of Profit and Loss',marks:10,tags:['Comparative P&L','Percentage change'],prompt:`Revenue is ₹20,00,000 in Year 1 and ₹25,00,000 in Year 2. Expenses are ₹15,00,000 and ₹20,50,000; tax is ₹1,00,000 and ₹90,000. Prepare a comparative statement showing absolute and percentage change for revenue, expenses, profit before tax, tax and profit after tax. Interpret the profit movement.`,steps:['Profit before tax: Year 1 ₹5,00,000; Year 2 ₹4,50,000. Profit after tax: Year 1 ₹4,00,000; Year 2 ₹3,60,000.','Changes: revenue +₹5,00,000 (+25%); expenses +₹5,50,000 (+36.67%); PBT −₹50,000 (−10%); tax −₹10,000 (−10%); PAT −₹40,000 (−10%).','Expenses grew faster than revenue, so profit fell despite higher sales. Always divide each percentage change by that line’s Year 1 amount.']},
{title:'Common-size Balance Sheet',marks:8,tags:['Common-size BS','Vertical analysis'],prompt:`Year 1 total assets are ₹10,00,000: non-current assets ₹7,00,000 and current assets ₹3,00,000. Year 2 totals are ₹12,00,000: non-current assets ₹7,20,000 and current assets ₹4,80,000. Year 1 equity, non-current liabilities and current liabilities are ₹5,00,000, ₹3,00,000 and ₹2,00,000. Year 2 amounts are ₹5,40,000, ₹3,60,000 and ₹3,00,000. Prepare common-size percentages for both sides and interpret the funding shift.`,steps:['Assets as % of total: non-current 70%→60%; current 30%→40%.','Equity 50%→45%; non-current liabilities 30%→30%; current liabilities 20%→25%. Each year’s side totals 100%.','The asset mix becomes more liquid on the face of the statement, but the current-liability share also rises. Examine the quality of current assets before calling liquidity stronger.']},
{title:'Common-size P&L versus absolute growth',marks:8,tags:['Common-size P&L','Margin'],prompt:`Year 1 revenue ₹16,00,000, cost of goods sold ₹9,60,000, operating expenses ₹4,00,000 and tax ₹60,000. Year 2 revenue ₹20,00,000, cost of goods sold ₹13,00,000, operating expenses ₹4,40,000 and tax ₹70,000. Construct the common-size lines for gross profit, operating profit and profit after tax and explain whether higher profit in rupees means better margins.`,steps:['Year 1: gross profit ₹6,40,000 (40% of revenue); operating profit ₹2,40,000 (15%); PAT ₹1,80,000 (11.25%).','Year 2: gross profit ₹7,00,000 (35%); operating profit ₹2,60,000 (13%); PAT ₹1,90,000 (9.5%).','PAT rises ₹10,000 in rupees, but all three margins decline. Common-size analysis reveals the squeeze hidden by absolute growth.']}
]}
];
